From d37719062f18653bfc83dadb94c594d808e9e313 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 20:26:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/22] util/u_thread: gate the thread-creation signal mask on
 pthread_sigmask
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

u_thread_create() blocks every signal but SIGSYS and SIGSEGV around
thrd_create(), and the block is guarded by HAVE_PTHREAD alone. That
conflates two different questions: whether the C library has threads,
and whether it has pthread_sigmask. POSIX puts pthread_sigmask in the
signal option group, not in threads, so a C library can implement all of
pthreads without it — and then this file does not link, on a function
every consumer of util/ reaches through u_queue.

Formulated as a property of the C library rather than as an OS name: a
configure check sets HAVE_PTHREAD_SIGMASK, and the guard tests it. Every
platform that has the function keeps the exact behaviour it had, because
the check answers yes there. The #else branch is not new — it is the
plain thrd_create() this reduces to, already in the file for the
platforms without pthreads — so a library without pthread_sigmask loses
the signal masking and nothing else.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 3 item 4 (threads — C11 threads over
  pthread/newlib)
Why: devkitA64's newlib/libnx has pthreads (dependency('threads') is
  found, HAVE_PTHREAD is set, pthread_create/mutex/cond/key/barrier all
  link) but no pthread_sigmask. Horizon has no POSIX signal delivery, so
  the masking has nothing to mask.
Evidence: configure reports
    Run-time dependency threads found: YES
  and an undefined-symbol audit of Mesa's built non-driver core against
  the whole devkitA64 + libnx + portlibs symbol set leaves
  pthread_sigmask unresolved, referenced by exactly one object:
    pthread_sigmask   : u_thread.c.o
  Reproduced directly:
    $ aarch64-none-elf-gcc -specs=.../switch.specs p_pthread_sigmask.c -lnx
    undefined reference to `pthread_sigmask'
  while the same probe links for sched_yield, clock_gettime,
  pthread_barrier_init and pthread_getcpuclockid, so this is one absent
  function and not a missing threading layer.
Upstream: yes — it is a libc trait, not an OS name; the fallback is the
  branch that was already there; and no platform that has the function
  changes behaviour.
---
 meson.build         | 11 +++++++++++
 src/util/u_thread.c |  8 +++++++-
 2 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/meson.build b/meson.build
index ef415aa..ef9c244 100644
--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -1792,6 +1792,17 @@ if dep_thread.found()
       args : '-D_GNU_SOURCE')
     pre_args += '-DHAVE_PTHREAD_MUTEX_TIMEDLOCK'
   endif
+  # util/u_thread.c blocks every signal around thread creation, which
+  # needs pthread_sigmask. POSIX makes it optional (it belongs to the
+  # signal option group, not to threads), so a C library can provide the
+  # rest of pthreads without it.
+  if cc.has_function(
+      'pthread_sigmask',
+      dependencies : dep_thread,
+      prefix : '#include <signal.h>',
+      args : '-D_GNU_SOURCE')
+    pre_args += '-DHAVE_PTHREAD_SIGMASK'
+  endif
 endif
 
 with_expat = get_option('expat') \
diff --git a/src/util/u_thread.c b/src/util/u_thread.c
index 3a8785c..e10d5b5 100644
--- a/src/util/u_thread.c
+++ b/src/util/u_thread.c
@@ -47,7 +47,13 @@ util_get_current_cpu(void)
 int u_thread_create(thrd_t *thrd, int (*routine)(void *), void *param)
 {
    int ret = thrd_error;
-#if defined(HAVE_PTHREAD) && !DETECT_OS_FUCHIA
+/* Creating the thread with every signal blocked needs pthread_sigmask,
+ * which POSIX makes optional and which a C library can leave out even
+ * though it has the rest of pthreads. Having it is therefore a property
+ * of the C library, not of having threads at all. The #else below is
+ * already the plain creation this reduces to, so a library without it
+ * loses the signal-masking and nothing else. */
+#if defined(HAVE_PTHREAD) && defined(HAVE_PTHREAD_SIGMASK) && !DETECT_OS_FUCHIA
    sigset_t saved_set, new_set;
 
    sigfillset(&new_set);
